<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>New cavitary, smear-positive pulmonary tuberculosis in a 21-year-old dentist who drinks heavily. Please confirm the regimen and doses, advise on liver monitoring given his alcohol use, and advise on isolation, return to dental work, and the length of treatment. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within two weeks, on the same day as his two-week primary care visit. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Do you agree with the regimen and the weight-based doses?</item><item>How should his liver be monitored, given his drinking, and when should the drugs be stopped?</item><item>When can he leave home isolation and return to treating patients?</item><item>How long should treatment last, given the cavity, and is the four-month rifapentine and moxifloxacin regimen an option?</item><item>When can ethambutol be stopped?</item><item>Is any other testing or follow-up needed from infectious diseases?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Holliday, a 21-year-old dentist, is seen at the request of Dr. Beaumont two weeks after a diagnosis of cavitary pulmonary tuberculosis. He has taken all thirteen doses of isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, and ethambutol with pyridoxine by video-observed therapy. His cough and night sweats are improving and the hemoptysis has stopped. He has mild nausea after the morning doses and orange urine and tears, and no jaundice, abdominal pain, rash, numbness, or vision change. He has cut his drinking down sharply. He has no travel outside the country, no known contact with drug-resistant tuberculosis, and no immune-suppressing condition or medicine. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>John Henry Holliday was born on August 14, 1851, in Griffin, Georgia. [D]</item><item>In 1866 Holliday&apos;s mother died of tuberculosis. [D]</item><item>He enrolled at the Pennsylvania College of Dental Surgery in 1870 and graduated in 1872, then practiced dentistry in Atlanta before opening his own practice in Griffin. [D]</item><item>Shortly after beginning his career as a dentist in Atlanta he developed a bad cough; doctors diagnosed tuberculosis and advised a move to a more arid climate. He developed chronic pulmonary tuberculosis. [D]</item><item>He was diagnosed with tuberculosis in 1873 and moved west for a healthier, drier climate, arriving in Dallas, Texas, in September 1873. [D]</item><item>He made his living at the card tables, aggravating his tuberculosis with heavy drinking and late nights. [D]</item></list><paragraph>1 later historical claim is omitted because it describes events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

